    plants: [
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Lodi",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 1. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 2,3,4,7,8, 11,14. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: Early blooming (biennial bearing), early summer apple; clear yellow, crisp flesh, fair quality. Fruit has short shelf life. Especially good for pies. Requires several pickings. (Sources-1,8,9,10). Source marks this cultivar as partially self-fruitful but still recommends a pollen source for good yields"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "State Fair",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 2. Hardiness zones: 4-5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8. Uses: Fresh Cooking. Source description: Juicy, moderately tart early apple; good for cooking and eating fresh; may exhibit biennial bearing, fire blight susceptible. (Sources-11,12,13)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Minnewashta",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 3. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,3,4,7,8, 10,13,15, 19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: High-quality, early season University of Minnesota introduction (formerly Minnewashta); fruit crisp, juicy, and tart. Very susceptible to scab. Keeps 6 to 8 weeks in common storage. (Sources-1,9). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer. Source table names this as Zestar"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Gala",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 4. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 4,5,6,8,11,12,13,14,15,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: Crisp, firm, aromatic; sweet and low acid; well-adapted and consistent cropper; bred in New Zealand; good storage quality. (Sources-1,4,5,6,9,10). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Wealthy",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 5. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: B-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,5,6,11,13,18,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: Very susceptible to Fire Blight disease. Hardy and long-lived tree. Fruit is medium, crisp, and good quality. Ripens bright red, but can be used weeks before ripening. (Sources-8,9,10). Source marks this cultivar as partially self-fruitful but still recommends a pollen source for good yields"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Honeycrisp",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 6. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,6,7,8,11,13,16,18,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: University of Minnesota introduction; very high fruit quality; tree only moderately strong; biennial bearing tendency; susceptible to bitter pit. (Sources-1,2,5,6,7,8,9,10)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Kindercrisp",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 7. Hardiness zones: 3-7. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,6,7,8,10,11. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: Reliable, cold-tolerant offspring of Honeycrisp. Fruit slightly smaller than Honeycrisp; excellent for kid's lunchboxes; flavor and crispness similar to Honeycrisp. (Sources-11,12,13)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Liberty",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 8. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,6,7,11,13,18,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: One of the new breed of disease-tolerant apples; resistant to Fire Blight, scab, powdery mildew, and Cedar-Apple Rust; fruit flavorful, medium sized with crisp flesh texture. (Sources-1,6,7,8,9,10). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Empire",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 9. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,6,10,11,13,16,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: Well-adapted vigorous tree; McIntosh type, but firmer; classic apple flavor; occasionally susceptible to late spring frost. (Sources-1,9,10). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Spartan",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 10. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,5,10,11,13,14,16,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: A firmer, attractive McIntosh type, with more color than McIntosh. May exhibit pre-harvest fruit drop. Excellent dessert quality. (Source-10). Source marks this cultivar as partially self-fruitful but still recommends a pollen source for good yields"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Honeygold",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 11. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,5,10,11,13,17,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: A hardy substitute for Golden Delicious. Susceptible to Fire Blight and cedar-apple rust. Medium to large size, golden to yellow-green. Flesh is crisp, yellow, juicy, and has a flavor similar to Golden Delicious. (Sources-5,8). Source marks this cultivar as partially self-fruitful but still recommends a pollen source for good yields"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Haralson",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 12. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,3,4,5,7,11,13,15,17. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: Very hardy tree that starts bearing young but has a biennial bearing habit. Medium-size fruit with red stripes; tart, fall apple that stays crisp and juicy through mid-winter. (Sources-2,8). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Jonathan and Jonathan types",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 13. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,3,4,7,8,12,14,15,17. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: Well-adapted but susceptible to Cedar-Apple Rust and Fire Blight. Excellent fall apple, good for cider making, excellent for pies and desserts. (Sources-1,4,9,10)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Northwestern Greening",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 14. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: B-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 4,7,8,10,11,14,15,19. Uses: Cooking. Source description: Culinary apple; large fruit with smooth, clear, pale yellow skin. Flesh is crisp and mild; good winter storage. (Source-8)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Golden Delicious",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 15. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-D. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,5,7,8,10,11,14,15,17,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert Applesauce. Source description: A yellow apple, excellent for both processing and fresh market. Tree is productive and must be thinned to achieve annual production. Susceptible to russeting. Fruit tends to shrivel in storage. (Sources-1,4,6,9,10)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Red Delicious",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 16. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-C. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,7,8,10,12,13,15,18,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert. Source description: A popular cultivar grown throughout the apple-producing areas of the country. No processing value, only fair fruit quality. Difficult to grow properly, strong tendency for biennial bearing. (Sources-1,4,5,9,10)"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Rome",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 17. Hardiness zones: 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-B. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,7,8,11,14,17,19. Uses: Cooking. Source description: Excellent baking and cooking apple, late blooming, pleasant flavor, firm. Keeps through mid-winter. (Sources-9,10). Source marks this cultivar as partially self-fruitful but still recommends a pollen source for good yields"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
            name: "Enterprise",
            category: "Apple cultivars for Nebraska",
            description: "Pollinizer number: 18. Hardiness zones: 4 5. Nebraska horticultural regions: A-E. Pollen-source cultivars: 1,2,3,4,5,10,13,14,15,17,19. Uses: Fresh Dessert Cooking. Source description: Disease resistant, stores up to 3 months at 1 C; juicy, firm, crisp, medium-red apple. (Sources-1,6,8,9). Source marks this cultivar as self-unfruitful and requiring a pollinizer"
        },
        {
            type: "Apple",
